Session 294 summary — a full RULE-4 cycle on D-503 completed: probe designed→landed→deployed→measured, collapse reproduced, hypothesis decided.

**Landed and deployed 0.11.506** (sv 289E4A3F4164D39FFC7DF90) with the P296-BASTQLAT probe: a per-inode stamp at both bast-work arm chokepoints plus an excess-latency check at both work-fn entries, measuring pure queue-to-run delay on the inode-BAST workqueue.

**The D-503 collapse reproduced on the probe build**: after 20 accumulated board cells (all PASS), crash_consistency went 0/32 NO_TERMINAL_RECORD and dir_reuse_coherency failed pace — the exact ledgered signature.

**The open hypothesis is DISPROVEN**: zero P296 events fired on any of the 32 nodes inside the crash_consistency fail window, and every node's log cap saturated only after its window closed, so the absence is genuine. The workqueue was not backlogged during the collapse — the sweep-release torrent does backlog it (up to ~900ms excess), but only in inter-test gaps.

**What replaced it, proven in-window**: the create path stalled 6.4–12.5 seconds per shared-dir EX acquire (P34-ACQ-SLOW, ino=26226559, four stalls in test17's 90s window) — one to two full 6000ms BAST-retry periods with the holder sitting on the grant. The prior discriminators (P50-RD ~4000 vs 500, P12-AGBAST-RX ~78 vs 10) reproduced exactly. A fleet-wide millisecond-resolution grant-chain timeline for the dir was collected to the scratchpad; the next step is naming the holder during the stall and why it ignored 1–2 BAST retry periods, then the RULE-5 consult.

